## std/sync/composition/cancel_token — CancelToken canonical wrapper (D.10 Phase 2)
##
## A capability-style cancellation handle that any holder can flip.
## Orthogonal to `Scope` (which is structural — parent cascades to
## children). Use a CancelToken when peer-to-peer cancellation is the
## right shape (a worker hands its parent a token so the parent can
## cut the worker off without owning its scope).
##
## Usage:
##
##   var tok = CancelToken.new()
##   go (-> { while not tok.is_cancelled() do_unit_of_work() end; 0 })
##   sleep_ms(500)
##   tok.cancel()
##   tok.free()
##
## Or with a deadline:
##
##   var tok = CancelToken.new_with_deadline(5000)   # auto-cancels in 5s
##
## D.10 canonical surface; the legacy `cancel_token_new()` /
## `cancel_token_cancel(h)` / `cancel_token_is_cancelled(h)` /
## `cancel_token_with_deadline(ms)` / `cancel_token_free(h)` free
## functions remain callable during the migration window.

struct CancelToken
  handle: Int
end

extend CancelToken
  ## Construct a new uncancelled token. Pass to any worker(s) that
  ## should observe `is_cancelled()` to stop early.
  def new(): CancelToken = CancelToken { handle: cancel_token_new() }

  ## Construct a token that automatically cancels itself after `ms`
  ## milliseconds (deadline-style cancellation).
  def new_with_deadline(ms: Int): CancelToken = CancelToken { handle: cancel_token_with_deadline(ms) }

  ## Flip the token to cancelled. Any holder can call this; the change
  ## is observable by all concurrent `is_cancelled()` readers.
  def cancel(self): Void = cancel_token_cancel(@handle)

  ## Check whether the token has been cancelled. Designed to be polled
  ## from worker loops.
  def is_cancelled(self): Bool = cancel_token_is_cancelled(@handle)

  ## Free the underlying state. Caller is responsible for ensuring no
  ## live worker still references the handle.
  def free(self): Void = cancel_token_free(@handle)
end
